{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01832gHHz76YSFo2AfuuYgme","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers\n3\tdescription: \"Re-drive a rework finding's already-landed fix instead of reading it; tightening one of two paired readers silently wedges the other\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: feedback\n7\t  originSessionId: 0c77d95a-257c-47ee-9c6a-bf0d0ac81ad1\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\tWhen a worker session inherits a REWORK FINDING that a prior commit on the same\n11\tbranch already fixed, **re-drive the fix rather than confirm it by reading the\n12\tdiff**. On WI-579 the round-022 MAJOR was closed at `c939d49c`; driving it on\n13\tthe suite's own fixture surfaced a NEW major defect the fix itself introduced.\n14\t\n15\tThe specific trap, which recurred three times on one lane (rounds 007, 015,\n16\t022→023): where two readers answer one question from two sides — the merge gate\n17\t(`integrate._verdict_gate`) and the loop's owed-derivation\n18\t(`agent_loop.review_owed_by_evidence`) — **tightening one silently wedges the\n19\tlane**, because the other still clears on the looser rule. The lane then\n20\tschedules nothing while the gate refuses, or draws a round the gate won't read.\n21\t\n22\t**Why:** a passing suite proves neither reader wrong; the disagreement is only\n23\tvisible when you drive BOTH at the same fixture state and compare answers.\n24\t\n25\t**How to apply:** write a scratch test that calls both readers on one fixture\n26\tand asserts they agree, before touching anything. Then put the shared rule in\n27\tthe leaf both import (here `kitlib/verdict.py`) — and when the two questions are\n28\tlegitimately different (drawn-vs-approved), say so in the docstring and pin the\n29\tdivergence class in a test, rather than forcing a false unity. Related:\n30\t[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]],\n31\t[[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]].\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5b2db2ef-e0c7-48d3-b559-2dce3466f1be","uuid":"2d31eb2d-343b-4715-addf-8c9abdf5f19b","timestamp":"2026-09-03T12:03:22.489Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/diytechy/.claude/projects/-Users-diytechy-Documents-ai-template/memory/verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers.md","content":"---\nname: verify-inherited-fixes-paired-readers\ndescription: \"Re-drive a rework finding's already-landed fix instead of reading it; tightening one of two paired readers silently wedges the other\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 0c77d95a-257c-47ee-9c6a-bf0d0ac81ad1\n---\n\nWhen a worker session inherits a REWORK FINDING that a prior commit on the same\nbranch already fixed, **re-drive the fix rather than confirm it by reading the\ndiff**. On WI-579 the round-022 MAJOR was closed at `c939d49c`; driving it on\nthe suite's own fixture surfaced a NEW major defect the fix itself introduced.\n\nThe specific trap, which recurred three times on one lane (rounds 007, 015,\n022→023): where two readers answer one question from two sides — the merge gate\n(`integrate._verdict_gate`) and the loop's owed-derivation\n(`agent_loop.review_owed_by_evidence`) — **tightening one silently wedges the\nlane**, because the other still clears on the looser rule. The lane then\nschedules nothing while the gate refuses, or draws a round the gate won't read.\n\n**Why:** a passing suite proves neither reader wrong; the disagreement is only\nvisible when you drive BOTH at the same fixture state and compare answers.\n\n**How to apply:** write a scratch test that calls both readers on one fixture\nand asserts they agree, before touching anything. Then put the shared rule in\nthe leaf both import (here `kitlib/verdict.py`) — and when the two questions are\nlegitimately different (drawn-vs-approved), say so in the docstring and pin the\ndivergence class in a test, rather than forcing a false unity. Related:\n[[rederive-dont-rerun-measured-claims]],\n[[stale-not-discharged-clauses-on-approved-rows]].\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}}}
